{"text": "Miguel A. Cardona\neT\n\n{Ej @teachcardona\n\nEducational Experience:\n\nExecutive Leadership: 093 Superintendent Certification _ 2012\nUniversity of Connecticut, Summa Cum Laude\nDoctorate in Educational Leadership 2011\nUniversity of Connecticut, Summa Cum Laude\nSixth Year Certificate: Educational Leadership 2004\nUniversity of Connecticut; Summa Cum Laude\nM.A. Ea. Bilingual Bicultural Education 2000\nUniversity of Connecticut; Summa Cum Laude\n: 1997\n\nB.A. Science: Teacher Certification, Minor: Fine Arts\n\nCentral Connecticut State University; Magna Cum Laude\n\nCareer Experience:\n\nAssistant Superintendent for Teaching and Learning \u2014 Meriden Board of Education\n\nAdjunct Professor\u2014University of Connecticut\n\n2015-Present\n\nOversee the instruction, teaching, leadership development and evaluation, and curriculum for approximately\n\n9000 students and a staff of 1000\nBuild capacity in district to view educators as greatest lever for equity\nConduct leadership development through systematic professional learning opportunities\nFacilitate the District Leadership Team through aligned goals setting from the Board goals to classroom goals\nCoordinate Curriculum Committee meetings with the Board of Education\nLeverage the performance and evaluation process as a tool for professional growth and development\nServe as a liaison to the greater Meriden community and State Commissions\n\n2014-Present\n\nTeach courses on leadership ranging from school improvement to culture and climate\nDevelop syllabi that connect theory and research to practice in urban education\n\nPerformance Evaluation Specialist - Meriden Board of Education 2013-2015\n\n.\nPrincipal,\n\nDeveloped systems and structures in collaboration with labor groups to implement the Meriden Teacher\nvaluation and Development Plan for teachers and SEED for administrators\nTrained district certified personnel in the evaluation plan, developing and aligning goals, and using the\ntechnology-based operating system.\nCT Professional Evaluation Advisory Committee (PEAC) member\n\nWanover Elementary School \u2014 Meriden Board of Education 2003-2013\nWith a committed team, produced amongst the district highest achievement gains in literacy and numeracy\nServed as instructional leader to an Early Intervention Pre-Kindergarten through Grade 5 School with\napproximately 550 students including 100 bilingual students and 85 staff members\nCollaborated with teachers, staff, and parents through the use of observation, assessment, and behavioral data, to\nprovide a safe and challenging academic program for all students\n\nArt Curriculum Supervisor \u2014 Meriden Board of Education 2004-2013\n\n\u00b0\ne\n\nCollaborated with art teachers to provide a meaningful art education program for all Meriden students\nDeveloped and revised the K-12 Art Curriculum for Meriden Public Schools\nPlanned professional development opportunities for teachers related to their discipline",
